    "no_js": {
      "kind": "no_js",
      "title": "Project knowledge, organized for AI.",
      "command": "git clone https://github.com/aDNA-Network/aDNA.git ~/aDNA && cd ~/aDNA && claude",
      "copyVisible": false,
      "text": "Open standard · MIT\n\nProject knowledge, organized for AI.\n\naDNA (Agentic DNA) is an open standard for organizing a project's files. Three folders and plain Markdown give people and AI agents a shared map.\n\nStart a local workspace with Git and Claude Code.\n\nGet Started\nRead the standard\n$\ngit clone https://github.com/aDNA-Network/aDNA.git ~/aDNA && cd ~/aDNA && claude\n\nNot a product or service — no server, no signup; aDNA itself sends nothing.\n\nThe standard that gives it that shape is open. Your files stay on your machine. Your AI tool has its own data handling.\n\nOpen source on GitHub\n·\nMIT-licensed\n·\nthe standard, versioned and public\nA project you can inspect\nFolder structure\naDNA.aDNA/\n├── CLAUDE.md      Agent instructions\n├── STATE.md       Work in progress\n├── what/          Knowledge and decisions\n├── how/           Plans and procedures\n└── who/           People and roles\nCLAUDE.md excerpt\n\nThe site you are reading uses this structure. Its instruction file tells an agent how to work:\n\n# CLAUDE.md — aDNA.aDNA\n\n## Standing Orders\n\n1. **Phase gates are human gates.** Never auto-advance between campaign phases without explicit user approval.\nRead the full instruction file\n\nThese are written instructions. The structure does not enforce an agent’s behavior."
    },
